   "source": [
    "Before we start filtering for specific business metrics like revenue, we need to ensure the basic structure of our dataset is sound. \n",
    "\n",
    "First, we check for and remove any duplicate rows. In accounting data, duplicates usually mean a system glitch caused a transaction to be recorded twice. If we leave them in, they will artificially inflate the transaction volume and total profit for certain states, which would bias our final analysis. \n",
    "\n",
    "Next, we drop any rows that contain missing values (`NaN`). This is a crucial preparation step for our predictive analytics later on. Machine learning algorithms, like the logistic regression we plan to use, require complete datasets. If we feed the model rows with missing tax rates or unknown states, it will simply throw an error. \n",
    "\n",
    "Finally, because removing duplicates and empty rows leaves gaps in our internal row numbering, we reset the index. This gives us a clean, continuous sequence of rows, preventing any data alignment issues in the upcoming steps."
   ]
  },
