   "source": [
    "## Clustering: Tax Profile and Profitability Across States\n",
    "\n",
    "In this section we group Trina's transactions using K-Means based on their tax profile\n",
    "(state, county, city and special tax rates). The goal is to understand whether the\n",
    "states can be separated into clearly distinct groups by their tax burden, and how these\n",
    "groups differ in their post-tax profit margin. This addresses Trina's core question of\n",
    "where she operates most profitably."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 72,
   "id": "32d86b3d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "['StateRate',\n",
       " 'EstimatedCountyRate',\n",
       " 'EstimatedCityRate',\n",
       " 'EstimatedSpecialRate',\n",
       " 'State_Arizona',\n",
       " 'State_Idaho',\n",
       " 'State_Montana',\n",
       " 'State_Utah',\n",
       " 'State_Washington',\n",
       " 'State_Wyoming']"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 72,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Safety step: remove whitespace in 'State' (in case it wasn't done during cleaning)\n",
    "df_clean[\"State\"] = df_clean[\"State\"].str.strip()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Features: state (categorical) + the four tax rates\n",
    "short = df_clean[[\"State\",\n",
    "                  \"StateRate\",\n",
    "                  \"EstimatedCountyRate\",\n",
    "                  \"EstimatedCityRate\",\n",
    "                  \"EstimatedSpecialRate\"]]\n",
    "\n",
    "# K-Means needs numbers -> convert the categorical column 'State' into dummy variables\n",
    "short_dummys = pd.get_dummies(short)\n",
    "short_dummys.columns.tolist()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d355b627",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "K-Means can only work with numerical values. We therefore convert the text column\n",
    "`State` into six 0/1 columns using `pd.get_dummies()` (e.g. `State_Montana`). The four\n",
    "tax rates all lie in a range of roughly 0 to 0.065 and are therefore naturally\n",
    "comparable, so no additional scaling is required."
   ]
  },
